{"kind":"report","text":"C2 pre-Source technical decision for65205531 underb860d58f/e029, implementing adopted directory-partition D4 section10. Proceed with the proposed narrow integer-index extension, subject to the following exact boundary. This is contract/implementation advice, not Source approval or partition activation.\n\n1. Allow int as the eighth indexed slot type only in platform data. Retain indexes' existing1..4 distinct slot names, refs-or-values restriction, fixed:true and required:true; a declared definition still cannot state indexes. Keep normal int field/slot validation: declared safe integer min/max, min\u003c=max, and actual number is safe, not negative zero, within its declared range. Do not impose positivity globally; negative values and zero are valid when the declaration permits them. Directory generation/number positivity belongs in future platform data. For a binding selector, preserve existing required field and exact stated type comparison, including matching int min/max, rather than weakening it to any integer.\n\n2. Reuse keyOf with canonicalize(number) unchanged: unquoted signed decimal text, 0 spelled0, no leading zeros/plus/exponent, no alternate encoder or numeric collation. String1 remains a different value/key from integer1. Existing local fact normalization can produce the same text as an integer position; indexes are scoped by item type and slot, whose fixed declared type controls their values. No global cross-type uniqueness is promised or needed. keyOf is generic and does not enforce a declaration's min/max; field/judgment validation must continue to do that before an item opens.\n\n3. Existing equality lookup semantics stay unchanged: key seek by type/slot/text, results ordered by item ID, bounded by caller limit; duplicate integer keys may name several live or final items. Preserve SELECTED_MOST and incomplete-index refusal; no range query, numeric ordering, implicit uniqueness, arbitrary collection or scan fallback. Fold writes the integer key with the opening item; later valid effects cannot change its fixed slot. Re-fold and checkpoint-item rebuild derive the same index, with no new index bytes in checkpoints.\n\n4. No additional integer-specific wire/schema/version discriminator is required for this platform-only additive validator change. The existing immutable platform definition name/version and its supported data/rules binding are the discriminator when a new platform cohort actually uses int indexes. Leave existing platform pins and their data/rules byte-identical in C2. Do not reinterpret an old named version to add indexes. Future directory@10/partition versions require their own explicit pin/version and Source review; old software may refuse an unsupported new cohort, never silently process it using old definitions. The internal platform validation option is no public opt-in for custom declared-definition indexes.\n\n5. Update the section17.2a contract/comment descriptions to name int and its canonical decimal key; replace the inaccurate seven-identity-only wording. Do not broaden FieldType grammar or change key/storage schema for this prerequisite. Keep C1/A1/D1, actual new directory/platform cohorts and partition routing outside this slice.\n\n6. Strengthen existing forms-binding witnesses at the cheapest meaningful boundary: an integer indexed platform slot accepted with permitted negative/zero/range endpoints; out-of-range and unsafe/fraction/-0 values rejected by actual admission, fixed/required and declared-definition restrictions retained, selector type/range mismatch still refused; distinguish integer1 from string1, equal duplicate keys and nearby distinct keys. Extend existing folding/checkpoint-rebuild case to integer keys with a later state/finality change leaving rows stable. One native SQLite/restart scenario should show admitted opening values, exact bounded lookup before/after restart and a prohibited fixed-slot change causing no item/index write. Preserve incomplete-index and duplicate-result limits; reuse their existing witnesses rather than adding a matrix of mirror tests. One focused control should distinguish removal of int admission or loss/wrong encoding of integer index rows, whichever meaningful invariant changes; then the required one frozen gate/exact-head review.\n\nIndependent read: current binding.ts, validate/binding.ts, validate/items.ts, field/slot validators, canonical writer, fold/index rebuild, MemoryState and SQLite lookup/write boundaries, existing forms-binding witnesses, D4 section10. A pure current-source probe (no edits) confirmed min/max safe integers,-10,0,1,10 produce exact decimal keys and pass broad int validation; -0,1.5 and unsafe integer produce no key and fail; text1 yields quoted key and fails int. This proves existing encoding/field boundaries only: current INDEXED_TYPES still lacks int and no new platform/runtime Source is approved.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","no_git_artifact":"true","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:418bc37d7b8e032a9f317074763cc628536b71d3","request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:652055311ae7333c64b7f2bfb1d1074d8c9bcf8e"}}